<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Michael Somare</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1936">1936</a>–<a href="/years/2021">2021</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Michael Somare was the founding prime minister of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papua-new-guinea">Papua New Guinea</a>. He helped found the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pangu-party">Pangu Party</a> in 1967, which pressed for self-government, became chief minister in 1972 and led the territory to self-government in 1973 and independence on 16 September 1975. He served as prime minister three times, for about seventeen years in all, and is called the father of the nation.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/michael-somare.jpg" alt="Michael Somare" width="306" height="459" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Michael_Somare,_1973_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Michael Thomas Somare was born on 9 April 1936 at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rabaul">Rabaul</a>, in a village called Rapikid, where his father Ludwig Somare Sana, a policeman who rose to sergeant, was stationed. He was the eldest of his father's six children. He grew up in the family village of Karau in the Murik Lakes district of East Sepik Province, and his first schooling was at a Japanese-run primary school there during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>. From 1946 he attended Boram Primary School, the Dregerhafen Education Centre and Sogeri High School, and in 1957 he gained a Leaving Certificate that served as a teaching qualification.</p><p>Somare stressed his Sepik identity, wearing a lap-lap rather than trousers, and held the title of Sana in his father's line, which marks descent from the clan's founder and the role of peacemaker. He married Veronica in 1965, and they had five children.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>Somare entered politics in 1968, sitting in the House of Assembly and then in the national parliament for the East Sepik seat. He was a founding member of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pangu-party">Pangu Party</a>, which led <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papua-new-guinea">Papua New Guinea</a> to independence in 1975. He was the first chief minister at the end of colonial rule and the first prime minister after independence. He held the office from 1975 to 1980, from 1982 to 1985 and from 2002 to 2011, seventeen years in all.</p><p>He was also minister of foreign affairs, leader of the opposition on several occasions and governor of East Sepik Province. As prime minister he declared friends with everybody and enemies of none as the principle of his foreign relations, and he opened relations with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> soon after independence. In March 2011, while he was in hospital in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/singapore">Singapore</a>, parliament declared the post vacant and Peter O'Neill became prime minister, a change that led to a constitutional crisis.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Somare died of pancreatic cancer at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/port-moresby">Port Moresby</a> on 25 February 2021, aged 84. He is widely called the father of the nation. He received honorary doctorates, beginning with one from the University of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a> in 1976, and was appointed to the Privy Council in 1977. He was made a Knight Grand Cross of the Order of St Michael and St George in 1990.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Michael Somare” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Michael Somare</b> was elected chief minister in 1972, the Whitlam government in Australia instituted self-governance in late 1973, and independence followed in 1975 as a Commonwealth realm.</blockquote><a href="/state/papua-new-guinea">Papua New Guinea</a></li><li><blockquote>The Pangu Party under <b>Michael Somare</b>, founded in 1967, pressed for self-government, achieved in 1973.</blockquote><a href="/state/territory-of-papua-and-new-guinea">Territory of Papua and New Guinea</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rabaul">Rabaul</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/papua-new-guinea">Papua New Guinea</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/port-moresby">Port Moresby</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/papua-new-guinea">Papua New Guinea</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=-4.2,152.17&amp;year=1991" aria-label="Rabaul on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Michael Somare"><image href="/maps/globes/g15000.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5185 5359h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5185 5359h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=-4.2,152.17&amp;year=1991">What was here in 1991? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
